<title>USA: U.S. March import prices fell 1.4 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. March import prices fell 1.4 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-04-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor Department report of</p>
<p>U.S. import and export price changes by percent (unadjusted).</p>
<p>    -			    Mar    Feb-Rev from  Mar 97/96</p>
<p> ALL IMPORTS		  -1.4   -0.8   -0.7     -1.6</p>
<p> -Petroleum		  -10.9   -7.5   -7.2     -1.2</p>
<p> -Nonpetroleum		-0.3   UNCH    0.1     -1.9</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	  2.8    1.9    1.8	4.0</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies     -4.7   -2.5   -2.2     -1.2</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -0.3   -0.5   -0.6     -5.3</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts    UNCH   UNCH   UNCH	0.1</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos  -0.6    0.2    0.2     -0.9</p>
<p>Percent changes, not seasonally adjusted --</p>
<p> ALL EXPORTS		  UNCH    0.2    0.1     -1.0</p>
<p> -Agricultural		 1.4    2.2    2.2     -6.2</p>
<p> -Non-Agricultural	  -0.1   UNCH   -0.1     -0.2</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	  1.5    2.0    2.1     -6.9</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies	0.1    0.2    0.1     -0.3</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -0.3   -0.1   -0.1     -0.5</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts     0.2   UNCH   UNCH	1.0</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos  -0.4    0.2   UNCH	0.5</p>
<p>The department reported March price indexes (1995 base year equals 100) of 99.4 for imports, and 99.6 for exports.</p>
<p>The department said the 1.4 percent drop in overall U.S. March import prices was the largest monthly decline since a 1.7 percent drop in December 1992.</p>
<p>It said the 10.9 percent drop in petroleum import prices was the biggest since an 11.0 percent decline in December 1993.</p>
</text>
